const p = (heading, type, ...paragraphs) => ({ heading, type, paragraphs });
const s = (heading, type, ...bullets) => ({ heading, type, bullets });

// Progressive rewrite of lib/data.js's uttarakhandGeography into explanatory paragraphs.
// Topics not yet listed here fall back to the original bullet version (see the merge
// in the page components) — this file only needs to contain the topics done so far.
export const uttarakhandGeographyExplained = {
  'Location & Extent': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Geographical Location & Extent',
    summary: 'Start with the state\'s position, boundaries, extent and strategic setting. These facts form the base for understanding climate, drainage, settlement and resources.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Location and boundaries', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand is a Himalayan state in northern India, lying in what government sources describe as the Central Himalayan zone. It extends roughly from 28°43′ N to 31°27′ N and 77°34′ E to 81°02′ E (different official sources round these endpoints slightly differently, so learn this commonly used range), covering a total geographical area of 53,483 km². Its four neighbours form a simple clockwise set worth remembering together: Uttar Pradesh to the south, Nepal to the east, China (Tibet) to the north and northeast, and Himachal Pradesh to the west — of these, only Nepal and China are international borders, which gives the state real strategic weight.'),
      p('2. Formation, and why the location matters', 'understand',
        'The state was carved out of the northern part of Uttar Pradesh on 9 November 2000, initially named Uttaranchal, a name changed to Uttarakhand a little over six years later, on 1 January 2007 — two separate dates that are easy to conflate but shouldn\'t be. Administratively it is organised into two major regions, Garhwal and Kumaon.',
        'What makes this location matter beyond the basic facts is the huge north-south relief range packed into a fairly narrow state: that range alone produces enormous variation in temperature, vegetation, agriculture and settlement from south to north, and gives Uttarakhand its importance for glaciers, river systems, forests, biodiversity, hydropower and tourism. The same steep slopes and deep valleys that create this variety also make the terrain fragile and prone to landslides and erosion, a theme that resurfaces across almost every other geography topic.'),
      s('3. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Area: 53,483 km²', 'Formation: 9 November 2000', 'Old name: Uttaranchal', 'Name changed to Uttarakhand: 1 January 2007',
        'Latitude: about 28°43′ N–31°27′ N', 'Longitude: about 77°34′ E–81°02′ E',
        'International neighbours: Nepal and China', 'National neighbours: Uttar Pradesh and Himachal Pradesh', 'Two major regions/divisions: Garhwal and Kumaon'),
      s('4. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Do not confuse the state\'s formation date (9 November 2000) with the later renaming to Uttarakhand (1 January 2007).',
        'Do not treat Nepal and China as national neighbours; they are international neighbours.',
        'Do not confuse geographical area with forest area or hilly area.',
        'For boundary questions, remember the simple clockwise set: UP → Nepal → China → Himachal Pradesh.'),
      s('5. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Uttarakhand = Himalayan state → 53,483 km² → formed 9 Nov 2000 → Uttaranchal renamed Uttarakhand in 2007 → borders UP, Himachal Pradesh, Nepal, China → Garhwal + Kumaon.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'Prepared from Uttarakhand government sources and aligned to the UKPSC geography syllabus.'
  },

  'Physiography': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Physiography of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'For Prelims, understand the major relief sequence and the relationship between altitude, terrain, climate, vegetation and human activity, rather than memorising every local landform.',
    sections: [
      p('1. The big picture', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand is overwhelmingly mountainous: rugged rock, deep valleys, high peaks and steep slopes dominate the landscape, and this relief is really the foundation for most of the rest of the state\'s geography. Altitude shapes temperature, temperature and moisture together shape vegetation, and vegetation in turn shapes agriculture and settlement — which is why the single most useful fact to fix before learning individual ranges, peaks and valleys is the south-to-north relief sequence: Terai, then Shiwalik, then Lesser Himalaya, then Greater Himalaya.'),
      p('2. The belts, south to north', 'understand',
        'At the southern edge, the Bhabar is a coarse, highly permeable piedmont belt right along the Himalayan foothills, porous enough that streams can actually vanish into it; the Terai lies just south of the Bhabar and is comparatively wetter, with finer sediments and more surface water, since it is here that the streams which disappeared in the Bhabar tend to re-emerge. The two are related foothill belts but shouldn\'t be treated as interchangeable.',
        'Moving north, the Shiwalik is the southernmost Himalayan range proper, young in geological terms and structurally fragile, with high erosion potential. The Lesser Himalaya is the middle mountainous zone, holding most of the state\'s valleys, settlements, agriculture, horticulture and tourism activity. And the Greater Himalaya is the highest belt, home to the tallest peaks, glaciers and snow-covered terrain, with only sparse settlement at its highest elevations.'),
      p('3. Why this sequence matters', 'understand',
        'This relief framework drives themes that recur throughout the rest of the syllabus: steep slopes encourage rapid runoff and deeply incised valleys, which is why relief controls drainage; altitude creates the vertical variation in climate and natural vegetation Uttarakhand is known for; and mountain relief limits where roads, settlements, agriculture and hydropower projects can actually be built, while making the state more exposed to landslides and erosion at the same time. It is worth resisting the temptation to memorise long lists of individual peaks and valleys before this basic sequence is solid, since the sequence and its consequences are what tends to be tested first.'),
      s('4. What to memorize', 'must',
        'South → north: Terai → Shiwalik → Lesser Himalaya → Greater Himalaya', 'Bhabar ≠ Terai',
        'Shiwalik = southern, young/fragile belt', 'Lesser Himalaya = middle mountainous belt with extensive human activity',
        'Greater Himalaya = highest belt, snow/glacier-dominated at high elevations'),
      s('5. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Do not reverse the Himalayan belt sequence.', 'Do not equate Bhabar with Terai.',
        'Do not assume the highest belt has the highest population density; harsh climate and terrain restrict settlement at high elevations.',
        'Do not memorise long lists of peaks and valleys before understanding the physiographic framework.'),
      s('6. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'South → North = Terai → Shiwalik → Lesser Himalaya → Greater Himalaya. Altitude ↑ → temperature generally ↓ → vegetation changes → agriculture/settlement changes → snow/glacier at the highest elevations.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'Prepared as a UKPSC-focused conceptual layer from the official Uttarakhand geography syllabus and Uttarakhand government geographic descriptions.'
  },

  'Climate': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Climate of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand shows strong climatic variation because elevation rises dramatically from the southern plains to the high Himalaya. Focus on the altitude–temperature relationship, monsoon rainfall, winter precipitation, relief effects, and how climate connects to vegetation, agriculture, rivers and disasters.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Why climate varies so much here', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand has no single climate — conditions vary sharply with altitude, relief and location, following one simple but important relationship: as altitude increases, temperature generally falls, and that single fact then drives changes in vegetation, agriculture and settlement pattern all the way up the slope. The southern plains and foothills stay comparatively warm year-round, while the higher Himalaya experiences severe winters and heavy snowfall even as lower valleys stay much milder. Altitude is the single most important control on climate here, though relief (which forces moisture-laden air upward), the direction a slope faces, and the state\'s two main weather systems, the southwest monsoon and western disturbances, all shape the picture further.'),
      p('2. The four seasons', 'must',
        'The year splits into four recognisable seasons: a cold winter from December to February, when temperatures drop sharply in the mountains and western disturbances can bring rain or snow; a warming pre-monsoon or summer from March to May, felt most in the plains and lower valleys; the main southwest monsoon season from June to September, by far the most important period for rainfall; and a post-monsoon phase in October-November as the rains taper off and temperatures start falling again toward winter.'),
      p('3. The southwest monsoon and orographic rainfall', 'understand',
        'Most of the state\'s annual rainfall arrives with the southwest monsoon between June and September, and the mechanism behind it is a textbook example of orographic rainfall: moist monsoon winds hit the Himalayan relief, are forced upward, cool as they rise, and release their moisture as they go. This is also why rainfall is never uniform across the state — elevation, slope orientation and local topography all change how much lift the air gets in any given spot. Combined with steep, fragile slopes, this heavy monsoon rainfall is also the main driver of landslides, flash floods and erosion in the hills.'),
      p('4. Western Disturbances and winter precipitation', 'understand',
        'Winter precipitation works differently, and comes mainly from Western Disturbances, weather systems that approach the subcontinent from the west and northwest rather than from the monsoon direction. These systems bring rain at lower and middle elevations and snow higher up, and the snow they deposit is what sustains the state\'s glaciers and much of its water availability through the rest of the year — a good reason not to assume all winter precipitation falls as snow, since altitude decides that too.'),
      p('5. How climate connects to the rest of the syllabus', 'understand',
        'Because climate here is really altitude in disguise, it ends up driving almost every other geography topic: it decides which vegetation zones exist at which elevation, which crops and horticultural produce can be grown where, how much water rivers carry from monsoon rain and glacier melt, how exposed different areas are to landslides and flash floods, when and where tourism is viable, and why dense permanent settlement thins out sharply above a certain elevation.'),
      s('6. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Main rainy season: Southwest Monsoon, June to September', 'Important winter weather mechanism: Western Disturbances',
        'Altitude ↑ → temperature generally ↓', 'Himalayan relief + moisture-bearing winds → orographic rainfall',
        'Heavy monsoon rainfall + steep slopes = greater landslide/flash-flood risk'),
      s('7. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Do not assume Uttarakhand has the same climate everywhere; altitude creates major climatic differences.',
        'Do not confuse the Southwest Monsoon with Western Disturbances: monsoon dominates summer rainfall, western disturbances matter most for winter precipitation.',
        'Do not assume all winter precipitation falls as snow; lower elevations may receive rain while higher elevations receive snow.',
        'Do not study climate as an isolated chapter — expect it connected to vegetation, agriculture, rivers, glaciers, tourism and disasters.'),
      s('8. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Uttarakhand climate = altitude + Himalayan relief + monsoon + western disturbances. Altitude ↑ → temperature ↓. June–September = main monsoon. Mountains force moist air upward → orographic rainfall. Western Disturbances → winter rain/snow. Climate controls vegetation, agriculture, river flow and settlement.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused climate notes structured around Uttarakhand\'s altitude, Himalayan relief, southwest monsoon, western disturbances and their geographical effects. Detailed climate-change issues are covered separately under Environment & Natural Resources.'
  },

  'Drainage & Rivers': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Drainage System & Rivers of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand is the source region of major Himalayan river systems, especially the Ganga and Yamuna. Focus on river-system hierarchy, origins, tributaries, confluences, the Panch Prayag, glacier–river associations, and Kumaon\'s separate river geography.',
    sections: [
      p('1. The big picture, and where the Ganga begins', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand\'s drainage network is dense and largely Himalayan in character, shaped by steep relief, glacier and snowmelt, and monsoon rainfall, and it separates into two dominant systems: the Ganga and the Yamuna. Within the Ganga system, two headstreams matter above everything else, Bhagirathi and Alaknanda, and the single most important river fact in the whole topic is what happens where they meet: Bhagirathi rises from the Gangotri Glacier at Gaumukh in Uttarkashi district, Alaknanda drains the high Himalaya around Badrinath and collects several major tributaries on its way down, and the two rivers join at Devprayag — it is only from Devprayag onward that the combined river is actually called the Ganga, not before.'),
      p('2. The Bhagirathi system', 'understand',
        'The Bhagirathi flows down through the upper Garhwal Himalaya from Gaumukh to Devprayag, picking up tributaries such as the Bhilangana, Asi Ganga, Jadh Ganga and Kedar Ganga along the way. Of these the Bhilangana matters most for the exam, both because it rises from the Khatling Glacier region and because the Tehri Dam, the state\'s best-known hydropower project, is built on the Bhagirathi itself.'),
      p('3. The Alaknanda system and the Panch Prayag', 'must',
        'The Alaknanda\'s headwaters lie in the glacier region above Badrinath, and as it descends it absorbs four major tributaries one after another, at four of the state\'s most famous confluences; together with its final meeting with the Bhagirathi, these make up the Panch Prayag ("five confluences"): Vishnuprayag, where the Alaknanda meets the Dhauliganga; Nandaprayag, where it meets the Nandakini; Karnaprayag, where it meets the Pindar; Rudraprayag, where it meets the Mandakini; and finally Devprayag, where it meets the Bhagirathi and the combined river becomes the Ganga. The sequence runs strictly upstream to downstream in that order, Vishnu, Nanda, Karna, Rudra, Dev, and is worth memorising as a single chain rather than five separate facts, since questions often test the order itself.'),
      p('4. Glacier–river associations worth fixing', 'must',
        'A handful of glacier-to-river links come up often enough to be worth knowing on their own: the Gangotri Glacier at Gaumukh feeds the Bhagirathi, the Chorabari Glacier area near Kedarnath feeds the Mandakini, the Khatling Glacier feeds the Bhilangana, the glaciers around Milam feed the Gori river system, and the Namik Glacier feeds the eastern Ramganga. For exam purposes these matching pairs matter more than any descriptive detail about the glaciers themselves.'),
      p('5. The Yamuna system', 'understand',
        'The Yamuna, the state\'s other great river system, rises in the Yamunotri region of Uttarkashi district and flows down along the western edge of Uttarakhand, making it especially relevant to Uttarkashi and Dehradun-region geography. Its most important Himalayan tributary by far is the Tons, which itself draws water from smaller streams such as the Supin and Rupin; the Tons drainage dominates the western part of the state and should not be confused with any river of the Alaknanda system further east.'),
      p('6. Kumaon\'s separate river geography', 'must',
        'Eastern Kumaon has its own river geography, built around the Kali (also called the Mahakali), which forms a long stretch of the international boundary with Nepal and becomes the Sharda once it leaves the hills near Tanakpur and enters the plains — Kali and Sharda are simply two names for different stretches of the same river, not two different rivers. Its tributaries include the Gori, the Dhauliganga (a separate river from the Alaknanda system\'s Dhauliganga) and the Saryu. The Gori, also called the Goriganga, rises from glaciers around Munsyari and Milam and joins the Kali at Jauljibi; the Saryu meets the Gomti at Bageshwar and eventually joins the Kali itself at Pancheshwar; and the eastern Ramganga, rising from the Namik Glacier, joins the Saryu at Rameshwar. Keep the eastern Ramganga distinct from the separate western Ramganga, whose catchment lies around the Doodhatoli region further west and which eventually becomes a tributary of the Ganga system. A few smaller rivers round out the map without needing much individual detail: the Kosi in Kumaon, the Nayar (or Nayyar) system of Garhwal, whose eastern and western branches join before reaching the Alaknanda, and the Asan, Song and Suswa, which drain the Dehradun valley.'),
      p('7. Drainage, hydropower and hazards', 'understand',
        'The same steep gradients and high discharge that make this drainage network so dense also give Uttarakhand its large hydropower potential, with the Bhagirathi, Alaknanda and their tributaries hosting most of the state\'s major projects — Tehri Dam on the Bhagirathi is the one river–project association worth fixing at this stage, with the rest covered separately under Hydropower. River valleys double as the natural corridors for settlements, roads, pilgrimage routes and agriculture, but the same relief that carries this water so efficiently also drives erosion, landslides and flash floods, particularly once heavy monsoon rainfall adds to the glacier and snowmelt already in the rivers.'),
      s('8. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Bhagirathi: Gangotri Glacier/Gaumukh; Bhagirathi + Alaknanda meet at Devprayag → Ganga',
        'Panch Prayag, upstream to downstream: Vishnuprayag (Dhauliganga), Nandaprayag (Nandakini), Karnaprayag (Pindar), Rudraprayag (Mandakini), Devprayag (Bhagirathi)',
        'Tehri Dam → Bhagirathi', 'Yamuna → Yamunotri; Tons → major Yamuna tributary',
        'Kali → Nepal border → Sharda downstream (from Tanakpur)',
        'Gori + Kali → Jauljibi; Saryu + Gomti → Bageshwar; Eastern Ramganga + Saryu → Rameshwar; Saryu + Kali → Pancheshwar'),
      s('9. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'The name "Ganga" begins only after Bhagirathi meets Alaknanda at Devprayag, not at Gaumukh.',
        'Do not reverse the Panch Prayag sequence.',
        'Mandakini joins at Rudraprayag (not Karnaprayag); Pindar joins at Karnaprayag (not Rudraprayag) — the two are commonly swapped.',
        'Eastern Ramganga (Kumaon, joins the Saryu) and western Ramganga (Garhwal, joins the Ganga system) are different rivers.',
        'Kali and Sharda are the same river under two names, not two different rivers.'),
      s('10. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Gaumukh → Bhagirathi. Bhagirathi + Alaknanda at Devprayag → Ganga. Panch Prayag: Vishnu–Dhauli, Nanda–Nandakini, Karna–Pindar, Rudra–Mandakini, Dev–Bhagirathi. Yamunotri → Yamuna; Tons → major tributary. Kali → Nepal boundary → Sharda downstream. Gori+Kali → Jauljibi. Saryu+Gomti → Bageshwar. East Ramganga+Saryu → Rameshwar. Saryu+Kali → Pancheshwar. Tehri Dam → Bhagirathi.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused drainage notes prepared from Uttarakhand government river, tourism, district and environmental sources. Detailed dams and hydropower projects are intentionally reserved for the separate Hydropower topic.'
  },

  'Glaciers & Lakes': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Glaciers & Lakes of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand contains some of the most important Himalayan glaciers and high-altitude lakes in India. Focus on glacier–river associations, the Garhwal vs Kumaon split, and lake–district associations rather than exact dimensions.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Why glaciers matter here', 'understand',
        'Glaciers are the high Himalaya\'s main freshwater store, and most of Uttarakhand\'s important rivers either rise directly from a glacier or draw heavily on glacial meltwater, especially through the warmer months — which is why glacier questions in this exam are almost always really river questions in disguise. What matters is which glacier feeds which river, not the glacier\'s exact length or area.'),
      p('2. The major named glaciers', 'must',
        'The single most important glacier to know is the Gangotri Glacier in Uttarkashi district, whose snout, Gaumukh, is where the Bhagirathi actually emerges — Gaumukh itself is not a separate glacier or river, just the point where the ice ends and the river begins, and the glacier lies within Gangotri National Park. In Kumaon, the Pindari Glacier near Bageshwar feeds the Pindar river, which later joins the Alaknanda at Karnaprayag, while further north the Milam Glacier, around Munsyari in Pithoragarh, feeds the Gori Ganga, which meets the Kali at Jauljibi. Back in Garhwal, the Khatling Glacier in Tehri district gives rise to the Bhilangana, a tributary that eventually joins the Bhagirathi, and the Chorabari Glacier area near Kedarnath feeds the Mandakini. A second tier of glaciers is worth recognising by region even without memorising much detail: Kafni and Sunderdhunga in the broader Bageshwar-Kumaon Himalaya, Ralam near Munsyari, and Dokriani (Dokriani Bamak) in the Garhwal Himalaya.'),
      p('3. The Nainital lake cluster', 'must',
        'Uttarakhand\'s lakes split naturally into two groups: a cluster of lower-elevation freshwater lakes concentrated around Nainital in Kumaon, and a scatter of high-altitude Himalayan lakes across Garhwal, usually tied to glaciers, pilgrimage or trekking. The Nainital cluster is worth learning as a set: Naini Lake itself sits at the centre of Nainital town and is the reason the area is often called Uttarakhand\'s "Lake District"; Bhimtal and Naukuchiatal lie nearby in the same district, the latter named for its nine corners; and Sattal, whose name means "seven lakes," is actually a connected group that includes lakes named Nal Damyanti, Panna (or Garud), Ram, Laxman, Sita, Purna and Sukh Tal. Khurpatal, in the same district, rounds out the cluster, and Nanak Sagar in Udham Singh Nagar is worth knowing separately as an artificial reservoir rather than a natural lake.'),
      p('4. Garhwal\'s high-altitude lakes', 'must',
        'Garhwal\'s lakes are more scattered and each tends to carry its own distinct association. Dodital, in the Uttarkashi region, is the source of the Asi Ganga, which later joins the Bhagirathi, and is also known for the Himalayan golden trout found in its waters. Roopkund, high in the Chamoli-region mountains along the Bedni Bugyal-Wan trekking route, is famous less for its geography than for the human skeletal remains scattered around it. Hemkund, also in Chamoli, is a glacial lake beside Hemkund Sahib, a major Sikh pilgrimage site in the Govindghat-Ghangaria-Valley of Flowers area. Deoria Tal, near Ukhimath in Rudraprayag district, is a popular trekking destination known for its Himalayan views, while Vasuki Tal, reached by trekking beyond Kedarnath, and Kedar Tal, near Gangotri in Uttarkashi, are two separate high-altitude lakes that are easy to confuse with each other and with Kedarnath itself, but shouldn\'t be. Nachiketa Tal (Uttarkashi region) and Ruinsara Tal (western Garhwal, Tons-Har-ki-Dun area) are worth recognising without much extra detail.'),
      s('5. Garhwal vs Kumaon at a glance', 'must',
        'Garhwal glaciers/lakes: Gangotri, Khatling, Chorabari; Dodital, Roopkund, Hemkund, Deoria Tal, Vasuki Tal, Kedar Tal.',
        'Kumaon glaciers/lakes: Pindari, Milam, Kafni, Sunderdhunga, Ralam; Naini Lake, Bhimtal, Naukuchiatal, Sattal, Khurpatal.'),
      s('6. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Gangotri Glacier → Gaumukh → Bhagirathi (Uttarkashi)', 'Pindari Glacier → Pindar → Kumaon/Bageshwar',
        'Milam Glacier → Gori Ganga → Munsyari/Pithoragarh', 'Khatling Glacier → Bhilangana; Chorabari region → Mandakini',
        'Dodital → Asi Ganga; Hemkund → Hemkund Sahib (Chamoli); Roopkund → skeletal remains'),
      s('7. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Gaumukh is the snout of the Gangotri Glacier, not a river or a separate glacier.',
        'Pindari feeds the Pindar, not the Bhagirathi; Milam feeds the Gori Ganga, not the Pindar.',
        'Sattal is a group of (seven) lakes, not one lake; Naukuchiatal is the nine-cornered one.',
        'Do not confuse Kedar Tal, Vasuki Tal and Kedarnath — three different places in the same general area.',
        'Nanak Sagar is an artificial reservoir, not a natural lake.'),
      s('8. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Gangotri → Gaumukh → Bhagirathi. Pindari → Pindar. Milam → Gori Ganga. Khatling → Bhilangana. Naini–Bhimtal–Sattal–Naukuchiatal = Kumaon lake cluster. Dodital → Asi Ganga. Roopkund = skeletal high-altitude lake. Hemkund = Chamoli + Sikh pilgrimage. Deoria Tal = Ukhimath/Rudraprayag. Vasuki Tal = Kedarnath region.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused notes prepared from Uttarakhand Government, Kumaon Division and Uttarakhand Tourism sources. Exact glacier dimensions and changing measurements are intentionally omitted because river/source/location associations are higher-value for prelims.'
  },

  'Soils': {
    priority: 'HIGH', title: 'Soils of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand\'s soils vary strongly with relief, altitude, parent rock, vegetation, rainfall and river deposition. Focus on the Terai–Bhabar contrast, how soil changes with altitude, agricultural suitability and soil erosion, rather than detailed soil classifications.',
    sections: [
      p('1. The Terai–Bhabar contrast', 'must',
        'Uttarakhand\'s soils change as sharply as its relief does, running from the deep depositional soils of the plains to the thin residual soils of the high mountains, shaped along the way by altitude, slope, rainfall, vegetation, parent rock and how much material the rivers have deposited. The clearest contrast worth fixing early is between the two foothill belts covered in Physiography: Bhabar, closer to the mountains, has coarse, highly porous deposits of sand, gravel, pebbles and boulders, porous enough that streams can vanish straight into it, while the Terai just south of it has finer, moister alluvium that makes it genuinely fertile and well suited to intensive farming, above all rice, wheat and sugarcane, with Udham Singh Nagar the district to associate most closely with this belt.'),
      p('2. Alluvial, mountain and forest soils', 'understand',
        'Moving inland, the river valleys and plains, particularly around Haridwar and Udham Singh Nagar, carry alluvial soils deposited directly by rivers and are generally the state\'s most agriculturally productive land; lower hill valleys can carry similar alluvial sandy soils on a smaller scale. Climb higher and the soil is increasingly formed in place, from the weathering of the underlying rock, rather than being deposited by water; these residual or mountain soils vary hugely in depth and fertility depending on slope, rock type, vegetation and how much erosion is removing the weathered material, and on the steepest slopes they stay thin because nothing is left in place long enough to develop.',
        'Much of the state\'s hill and mountain area carries forest soils instead, whose character shifts with altitude, vegetation and climate: brown forest soils, often shallow and gravelly with a good deal of organic matter from decomposed vegetation, dominate under typical Himalayan forest, while podzolic soils, associated with cooler, more humid, coniferous conditions at higher elevations, form through active leaching driven by the wetter, cooler climate. At the very top, sub-alpine and alpine soils take over, shaped by low temperature, snow cover, sparse vegetation and a short growing season, including the meadow soils that sit beneath the high bugyals, and agricultural possibility narrows steadily as this altitude climb continues.'),
      p('3. The altitude ladder, and what grows where', 'must',
        'Put together, this gives a fairly predictable ladder running from the plains to the peaks: fertile alluvial soils in the Terai and plains, coarse porous deposits in the Bhabar, alluvial sandy or residual sandy-loam soils in the lower hills, sandy-loam and forest soils through the middle hills, red-to-dark mountain soils higher up, and cold-climate alpine and darker clayey soils at the very top — the exact pattern varies locally, but this general sequence is worth having ready as a single mental model.',
        'This soil ladder maps fairly closely onto what actually gets grown where: the Terai and plains support intensive cultivation of rice, wheat, sugarcane, pulses and oilseeds; lower-hill sandy-loam soils support cereals, pulses, vegetables and fruit; the middle and higher hills support finger millet, wheat, potato, pulses and horticulture; and the coolest high-altitude areas suit temperate fruit such as apple, pear, peach and plum wherever the other growing conditions line up too. Soil is only one input among several, though — altitude, climate, rainfall, irrigation and slope all matter just as much in deciding what a given patch of land can actually grow.'),
      p('4. Erosion and conservation', 'must',
        'Steep slopes make mountain soil highly vulnerable to erosion, and heavy monsoon rainfall can strip exposed topsoil quickly, a risk made worse by deforestation, road cutting and poorly managed construction that leave slopes with even less protection; the result is both lower agricultural productivity on the slope itself and more sediment ending up in the rivers below. Erosion and landslides are closely related but are not the same process: erosion removes soil gradually, while a landslide is a sudden mass movement of earth and rock downslope. The standard responses are to keep forest and vegetation cover intact, terrace the land to reduce the effective slope and slow runoff, farm along the contour and use bunding, and manage whole watersheds rather than isolated slopes, all more important in Uttarakhand than in most states, because so much of its agriculture and settlement sits on exactly this kind of fragile mountain terrain.'),
      s('5. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Terai → fertile alluvial soil (rice, wheat, sugarcane); Bhabar → coarse, gravelly, porous soil',
        'River valleys/plains → alluvial soils; mountain slopes → residual/forest soils',
        'Brown forest soil → Himalayan forests; Podzolic soil → cool, humid, higher-altitude/coniferous; Alpine soil → very high elevations/meadows',
        'Steep slope + monsoon rainfall → severe erosion risk'),
      s('6. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Bhabar is not wetter than Terai — Bhabar is porous and dry, Terai retains more surface moisture.',
        'Not all Himalayan soils are infertile; fertility depends on location, depth, vegetation and management.',
        'Podzolic soils belong to cool, humid, higher-altitude environments, not the hot Terai plains.',
        'Soil erosion (gradual removal of soil) is not the same process as a landslide (sudden mass movement).'),
      s('7. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Terai = fine, moist, fertile alluvium. Bhabar = coarse, gravelly, porous. Valleys/plains = alluvial soils. Hills = residual + forest soils. Higher cool humid areas = podzolic tendencies. Very high areas = alpine/meadow soils. Steep slopes + monsoon = erosion. Terracing + vegetation = conservation.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused notes prepared using Uttarakhand Government agro-climatic information and the Uttarakhand State of Environment Report. Detailed soil-profile chemistry and specialist classifications are intentionally excluded because they are lower-yield for Upper PCS prelims.'
  },

  'Natural Vegetation & Forests': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Natural Vegetation & Forests of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand shows pronounced vertical zonation of vegetation because altitude, temperature, rainfall and slope change rapidly from the Terai to the high Himalaya. Focus on the altitude–species sequence, dominant species at each belt, alpine meadows (bugyals), and forest fires.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Altitude as the master control', 'must',
        'Uttarakhand packs an extraordinary range of vegetation into a fairly short vertical distance, because altitude, and the temperature drop that comes with it, is the single strongest control on what grows where, with rainfall, slope aspect, soil and human activity all modifying the picture locally. The resulting sequence, moving from the Terai-Bhabar up to the highest peaks, runs: tropical and moist deciduous forest in the lowest belt, subtropical pine forest from roughly 1,000-2,000 m, Himalayan moist temperate forest from about 2,000-3,000 m, sub-alpine forest from about 3,400-4,000 m, and alpine vegetation and meadows above the tree line. These altitude bands are approximate rather than fixed boundaries, but the sequence itself is one of the highest-value things to know in this whole topic.'),
      p('2. The lower belt: Sal country', 'understand',
        'At the bottom of that ladder, the Terai, Bhabar and Shiwalik foothills carry warm, relatively moist tropical and mixed deciduous forest, and Sal is by far the most important and characteristic tree of this belt, alongside species such as shisham, khair and haldu. Sal belongs firmly down here, in the lower, warmer elevations, not anywhere near the temperate or alpine Himalaya.'),
      p('3. The subtropical belt: Chir pine and fire risk', 'must',
        'Moving up into the lower and middle Himalayan slopes, Chir pine takes over as the dominant species of the drier, sunnier subtropical belt around 1,000-2,000 m. It matters for more than just its place on the altitude ladder: Chir pine needles accumulate as dry litter on the forest floor and become highly flammable in dry conditions, which is why this belt is so closely tied to the state\'s recurring forest-fire problem — a problem, worth noting, that is driven as much by human activity as by natural causes.'),
      p('4. The temperate belt: oak, deodar, rhododendron', 'must',
        'Higher still, in the cooler, moister conditions of roughly 2,000-3,000 m, Himalayan moist temperate forest takes over, and it is genuinely more varied than the lower belts. Oak, particularly Banj oak, and Deodar are the two species worth knowing individually: oak for its ecological role in soil protection and water retention in relatively moist middle-Himalayan conditions, Deodar as a culturally and economically important conifer of slightly higher, drier temperate ground. Rhododendron arboreum, known locally as Buransh, is the other defining plant of this belt, valued for its bright red flowers, used in local beverages and products, though other rhododendron species also occur higher up in different forms.'),
      p('5. Higher up: fir, spruce, sub-alpine and alpine', 'must',
        'Climb further and fir and spruce become the dominant conifers of the higher temperate belt, adapted to colder conditions than anything lower down and marking the approach to the sub-alpine zone proper, placed at roughly 3,400-4,000 m. Vegetation here grows shorter and tougher, adapted to cold, wind and snow, and includes birch, commonly called Bhojpatra, whose bark was historically used as a writing material, along with juniper and high-altitude rhododendrons. Above the tree line, conditions turn too harsh for anything resembling a forest, and vegetation shifts to grasses, herbs, dwarf shrubs and flowering alpine plants surviving a short growing season, an ecosystem extremely sensitive to both climatic and human disturbance, and home to many of the medicinal and aromatic plants the state is known for. The high-altitude meadows that develop above or near the tree line are called bugyals, Bedni Bugyal, Dayara Bugyal and the Auli-region meadows are the best-known examples, and it is worth remembering that a bugyal is a meadow, not a forest, used seasonally for grazing as well as being important for biodiversity and tourism.'),
      p('6. Why forests matter beyond the species list', 'understand',
        'Forest roots and litter reduce surface runoff, stabilise soil and help retain moisture, which is why forested catchments matter so much for springs, streams and watershed behaviour, and why losing forest cover tends to worsen erosion and slope instability, though forests alone cannot prevent landslides since geology, slope, rainfall and construction all matter too. Mountain communities also depend directly on forests for fodder, fuelwood, timber and medicinal plants, part of why the state has promoted its identity as a "Herbal State." Over-extraction, grazing pressure and infrastructure development add a slower form of degradation on top of the fire risk already noted, which is why conservation has to extend beyond formally protected areas rather than stopping at their boundaries.'),
      s('7. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Altitude sequence: Sal (lower foothills/Terai-Bhabar) → Chir pine (subtropical) → Oak + Deodar (temperate) → Fir + Spruce (higher temperate) → Birch/Bhojpatra + Juniper (sub-alpine) → Bugyal/alpine meadow (above tree line)',
        'Buransh = Rhododendron arboreum', 'Chir pine needles → high forest-fire relevance', 'Forests → soil + water + biodiversity + livelihoods'),
      s('8. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Do not place Sal in the alpine or sub-alpine zone; it belongs to the lowest, warmest belt.',
        'Do not confuse Chir pine (subtropical, drier) with Deodar (temperate, higher).',
        'A Bugyal is a high-altitude meadow, not a forest.',
        'Altitude ranges for each belt are approximate, not rigid; slope aspect and rainfall modify them locally.',
        'Uttarakhand\'s forests are not only pine; oak and mixed temperate forest are ecologically just as important.'),
      s('9. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Sal (low) → Chir pine (subtropical) → Oak + Deodar (temperate) → Fir + Spruce (higher temperate) → Birch + Juniper (sub-alpine) → Bugyals/alpine (above tree line). Buransh = Rhododendron. Chir pine belt = fire-prone. Forests protect soil and water and support livelihoods.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused notes prepared from Uttarakhand Biodiversity Board and Uttarakhand government environmental material. Altitudinal ranges are approximate ecological belts rather than rigid boundaries; detailed biodiversity and protected-area facts are reserved for the National Parks & Wildlife section.'
  },

  'Agriculture & Horticulture': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Agriculture & Horticulture of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Agriculture varies sharply between the irrigated plains and the rain-fed hills. Focus on the plains-vs-hills contrast, traditional hill crops (Mandua, Jhangora, Barahnaja), the altitude split between subtropical and temperate fruit, and off-season vegetables.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Plains vs hills, and the altitude-zone framework', 'must',
        'Agriculture in Uttarakhand splits sharply along the same plains-hills line that runs through the rest of its geography. The plains of Haridwar and Udham Singh Nagar have large, well-irrigated holdings and genuinely intensive farming, while hill agriculture is defined by small, fragmented, terraced fields that depend far more on rainfall than on irrigation, and because only a limited share of this mountainous state can be cultivated at all, making efficient use of that land matters more here than in most states. Within the hills, a fairly standard set of agro-climatic zones tracks altitude: up to about 1,000 m covers Terai, Bhabar and lower-hill farming; 1,000-1,500 m is middle-hill agriculture; 1,500-2,400 m is high-hill agriculture and temperate horticulture; and above about 2,400 m farming has to work around a short growing season. As with vegetation, this is essentially altitude deciding what a farmer can grow.'),
      p('2. Major field crops and traditional hill crops', 'must',
        'Rice and wheat are the state\'s two major food crops overall: rice needs a warm season and adequate water, usually meaning the plains or well-watered valleys, though it is also grown in terraced hill fields wherever enough water is available, while wheat, a rabi crop sown after the monsoon, is cultivated widely in both the plains and the hills. Sugarcane belongs firmly to the warmer plains, concentrated in Haridwar and Udham Singh Nagar, and should never be paired with high-altitude farming.',
        'The hills have their own distinct traditional crops built for rain-fed mountain conditions: Mandua, the local name for finger millet (ragi), tolerant of difficult conditions and valued nutritionally; and Jhangora, barnyard millet, a short-duration crop suited to the same difficult terrain — the two are easy to mix up but are different millets. Amaranth (locally Ramdana), buckwheat, maize, pulses and soybean round out the traditional hill cropping mix depending on altitude. A distinctive feature of hill farming is Barahnaja, literally "twelve grains" though the actual number and mix varies field to field: several cereals, millets, pulses and oilseeds grown together in one plot, which spreads risk and makes the most of limited land. Terrace farming, converting steep slopes into level steps, is the physical adaptation that makes any of this cultivation possible on Himalayan gradients, and it doubles as soil and water conservation.'),
      p('3. Horticulture: temperate fruit versus subtropical fruit', 'must',
        'Horticulture is where Uttarakhand\'s altitude range turns into a genuine advantage rather than just a constraint, since a fairly small area can host tropical, subtropical and temperate growing conditions all at once, often giving higher returns per unit of scarce mountain land than field crops can. Lower elevations, the warmer valleys, foothills and plains, grow mango, litchi, guava, citrus and aonla, with Dehradun carrying a particularly strong traditional association with litchi. Climb into the cooler middle and higher hills and the fruit basket changes completely, to apple, pear, peach, plum, apricot and walnut; apple in particular needs real cold and is associated with the higher of these elevations, promoted today through the state\'s Mission Apple programme. The simplest way to hold this in your head is the contrast itself: lower altitude means mango and litchi, higher altitude means apple and the other temperate fruits.'),
      p('4. Vegetables, spices, flowers and protected cultivation', 'understand',
        'The same cool hill climate that grows temperate fruit also lets Uttarakhand produce vegetables, potato, tomato, peas, cauliflower, cabbage, onion and capsicum among them, during seasons when the plains are too hot, creating a real off-season market advantage that the state horticulture department actively promotes. Spices such as ginger, garlic, turmeric and chilli, and commercial flowers such as marigold, gladiolus, rose, gerbera and carnation, add further high-value options for small hill farms, and polyhouses and greenhouses (protected cultivation) are increasingly used to manage temperature and moisture directly and stretch growing seasons further for vegetables, flowers and nursery crops.'),
      p('5. Organic farming, irrigation, migration and value addition', 'understand',
        'Because hill farming has traditionally used few external chemical inputs, it has real potential for organic and specially branded produce, and interest in traditional crops like millets, pulses and amaranth has revived accordingly, partly for nutrition and partly for market demand. Irrigation availability stays deeply uneven, concentrated in the plains, which leaves hill farming reliant on rainfall, springs, streams and small local water-harvesting systems instead. Out-migration compounds these constraints by shrinking the available workforce and leaving fields abandoned in many villages, a pattern worsened by wildlife damage to crops, fragmented holdings and poor market access; the fuller migration picture is covered separately under Migration. The state\'s policy response leans heavily on value addition: since fruit and vegetables are perishable, better storage, processing and cold-chain infrastructure, turning raw produce into higher-value processed goods, is treated as central to making horticulture actually pay for hill households.'),
      s('6. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Plains agriculture → Haridwar + Udham Singh Nagar; hill agriculture → terraces, rain-fed, small holdings',
        'Rice + wheat = major food crops; sugarcane = plains only',
        'Mandua = finger millet; Jhangora = barnyard millet; Barahnaja = traditional mixed cropping ("twelve grains")',
        'Lower elevations → mango, litchi (Dehradun); higher elevations → apple, pear, peach, plum, apricot, walnut',
        'Hill climate → off-season vegetable advantage; polyhouse → protected cultivation'),
      s('7. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Uttarakhand agriculture is not only hill agriculture — Haridwar and Udham Singh Nagar have extensive plains farming.',
        'Do not confuse Mandua (finger millet) with Jhangora (barnyard millet).',
        'Apple belongs to the cooler hills, not the Terai; sugarcane belongs to the plains, not the hills.',
        'Barahnaja does not mean exactly twelve crops in every field — the composition varies.',
        'Horticulture covers fruit, vegetables, spices and flowers, not fruit alone.'),
      s('8. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Plains = irrigation + rice/wheat/sugarcane. Hills = terraces + rain-fed mixed farming. Mandua = finger millet. Jhangora = barnyard millet. Barahnaja = traditional mixed cropping. Low altitude = mango/litchi (Dehradun). Higher hills = apple/pear/peach/plum. Hill climate = off-season vegetables.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused notes prepared primarily from Uttarakhand Horticulture Department agro-climatic information and state environmental material. Production rankings and annual output figures are intentionally excluded because they change over time and should be studied through current affairs when relevant.'
  },

  'Minerals & Resources': {
    priority: 'HIGH', title: 'Minerals & Natural Resources of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand has a modest but specific set of mineral resources on top of its more important forest, water and biological resources. Focus on the major minerals (magnesite, limestone, base metals), minor minerals, riverbed minerals, and the environmental issues linked with mining.',
    sections: [
      p('1. The major minerals', 'must',
        'Uttarakhand\'s mineral wealth is modest by national standards but worth knowing precisely because it\'s so specific: the state government identifies magnesite, limestone and base metals as its major minerals, alongside a set of minor, more local resources and the sand, gravel and boulders that Himalayan rivers deposit along their beds. Magnesite, a magnesium carbonate mineral found in association with dolomitic rocks, is used mainly in refractories and magnesium-related industry, and its main Uttarakhand occurrence is the Bageshwar-Almora belt in Kumaon. Limestone, mostly calcium carbonate, is the other major mineral, valued as raw material for cement and lime, though large-scale extraction carries real environmental cost on this kind of fragile mountain terrain. Metallic occurrences of copper, lead and related base metals, along with mentions of gold and silver, exist in the Himalayan geology of both Kumaon and Garhwal, and copper in particular has a genuine history here, once used for utensils and local metalwork, but these occurrences are mostly scattered and limited today rather than commercially significant — Uttarakhand should not be thought of as a major iron-ore or coal state.'),
      p('2. Minor minerals', 'understand',
        'A tier of minor minerals sits alongside the major ones, worth recognising individually rather than in detail. Soapstone (also called steatite, a talc-rich rock) occurs together with dolomite and magnesite, with Bageshwar again the main mining region, and finds use in cosmetics, paper, ceramics, paint and detergents. Silica sand, valued for glass and foundry industries, and barite, a barium-bearing mineral used mainly in drilling fluids, round out the minor-mineral list; for prelims purposes it is enough to recognise each as a resource of the state rather than know their deposit chemistry.'),
      p('3. Riverbed minerals and building stone', 'must',
        'Because Himalayan rivers move so much coarse material down steep gradients, sand, gravel and boulders scraped from riverbeds are treated as minerals in their own right, and they matter economically as the raw material behind most local construction. The same steep, high-energy rivers that give the state its hydropower potential are what keep depositing this material in the first place, which is why riverbed mining connects directly back to Drainage & Rivers and to disaster geography: pulled out carelessly, it can reshape river channels and worsen erosion. Locally quarried stone and slate, long used for houses, roofs and retaining walls, add a further layer of building material, though not every such local rock should be treated as a nationally significant commercial mineral.'),
      p('4. The bigger resources: forests, water, medicinal plants', 'understand',
        'Beyond minerals proper, Uttarakhand\'s biggest resources are really its forests, water and medicinal plants. Forests supply timber, fuelwood, fodder and non-timber produce on top of their ecological role in water regulation and soil protection (covered fully under Natural Vegetation & Forests). Water, drawn from glaciers, snowfields, rivers, springs and the monsoon, makes the state the upper catchment of both the Ganga and Yamuna systems and underpins drinking supply, irrigation, hydropower and tourism, even though many mountain settlements still face local shortages and spring depletion despite this apparent abundance. And the same altitude range that shapes vegetation also supports a rich stock of medicinal and aromatic plants in the temperate, sub-alpine and alpine zones, valuable for local livelihoods and herbal industry but vulnerable to overharvesting. Hydropower itself, built on the state\'s steep gradients and perennial river flow, is covered in full under its own topic.'),
      p('5. Mining, environment and regulation', 'must',
        'Because the same terrain that holds these resources is also unusually fragile, mining and quarrying in Uttarakhand carry environmental costs beyond what similar activity would cost in flatter states: removing vegetation and cutting into slopes can trigger erosion and instability, riverbed mining can alter channels and sediment flow, and the dust, noise and transport that come with extraction affect nearby settlements and ecosystems. The state\'s Directorate of Geology and Mining handles exploration, administration and regulation, including rules specifically aimed at illegal extraction and transport, since demand for construction material makes riverbed minerals a particular target; the details of these rules change periodically, so treat any specific regulation as something to verify against current news rather than as fixed background fact.'),
      s('6. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Major minerals: magnesite + limestone + base metals; minor minerals: soapstone + silica sand + barite',
        'Magnesite and soapstone → Bageshwar/Kumaon belt; limestone → cement/lime industry',
        'Riverbed minerals: sand + gravel + boulders', 'Uttarakhand is not a major coal or iron-ore state'),
      s('7. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Do not treat coal as a major mineral resource of Uttarakhand.',
        'Do not confuse soapstone (talc-rich) with limestone (calcium carbonate).',
        'Do not forget sand, gravel and boulders when asked about mineral resources.',
        'Do not equate a mineral\'s presence with large-scale commercial production.'),
      s('8. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Magnesite + limestone = major mineral anchors. Soapstone = talc/steatite, Bageshwar/Kumaon. Silica sand + barite = minor minerals. Sand + gravel + boulders = riverbed minerals. Forests + medicinal plants = major biological resources. Himalayan mining requires ecological regulation.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused resource notes prepared primarily from the Uttarakhand Directorate of Geology and Mining. Detailed mine-wise reserves and changing production statistics are intentionally excluded; these should be added only when supported by PYQs or current-affairs relevance.'
  },

  'Hydropower': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'Hydropower of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand has major hydropower potential because of its steep Himalayan relief, deep valleys and perennial rivers. Focus on the major projects, their rivers, capacities, operating agencies, and the environmental issues that come with building dams in this terrain.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Why the potential is so high, and the three project types', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand\'s hydropower potential comes directly from its geography: Himalayan rivers drop rapidly from great heights, generating a large hydraulic head, and rivers such as the Bhagirathi, Alaknanda, Yamuna, Tons, Dhauliganga and Sharda/Kali keep flowing year-round on a mix of snowmelt, glacier melt and monsoon rain, while the state\'s deep valleys offer good sites for dams, barrages, tunnels and underground powerhouses. Projects here fall into three basic types worth distinguishing: storage projects, which hold water behind a dam and release it as needed; run-of-river projects, which use the river\'s natural flow with only limited storage; and pumped-storage projects, which pump water up to a reservoir during low-demand periods and release it to generate power at peak demand. It is worth remembering that even a "run-of-river" project, despite needing less storage, can still involve barrages, tunnels and diversion structures substantial enough to have real environmental impact.'),
      p('2. The Tehri Hydro Power Complex', 'must',
        'The state\'s flagship project by far is the Tehri Hydro Power Complex on the Bhagirathi in Tehri Garhwal district, developed by THDC India Limited, which brings together three components adding up to 2,400 MW: the Tehri Hydro Power Project itself, a 1,000 MW storage scheme built as an earth-and-rockfill dam and also serving as a multipurpose project for irrigation, drinking water and flood moderation; Koteshwar, a 400 MW station just downstream on the same river; and the Tehri Pumped Storage Plant, a 1,000 MW scheme (four reversible units of 250 MW each, fully commissioned as of April 2026) that uses the Tehri reservoir as its upper reservoir and the Koteshwar reservoir as its lower one, making it especially useful for balancing the grid as more solar and wind power comes online elsewhere.'),
      p('3. Other Bhagirathi and Alaknanda projects', 'must',
        'Further upstream on the Bhagirathi, in Uttarkashi district, sit the two Maneri Bhali stages: Stage I, 90 MW, built around the Maneri Dam with its powerhouse at Tiloth, and Stage II, 304 MW, with its powerhouse at Dharasu. On the Alaknanda in Chamoli district, THDC is also developing the 444 MW Vishnugad Pipalkoti project, which remained under construction as of September 2026.'),
      p('4. Kumaon and the border rivers: NHPC\'s projects', 'must',
        'Kumaon\'s main hydro project is NHPC\'s Dhauliganga Power Station in Pithoragarh district, 280 MW from four units of 70 MW each, a run-of-river scheme with limited pondage on the Dhauliganga river. NHPC also runs the Tanakpur Power Station near Banbasa in Champawat district, on the Sharda river (called the Mahakali across the border in Nepal), with a current derated capacity of 94.2 MW.'),
      p('5. The Yamuna-Tons chain and UJVN Limited', 'must',
        'Western Uttarakhand has its own chain of development on the Yamuna and Tons systems, mostly around the Dakpathar-Dehradun area and mostly run by UJVN Limited, the state\'s own hydropower company, formed in 2001. On the Tons itself, Chibro is an underground run-of-river station of 240 MW (four units of 60 MW). On the Yamuna, Vyasi, at Hathiyari in Dehradun district, generates 120 MW from two units of 60 MW, while Lakhwar, still under development in the same district, is planned at 300 MW as part of a wider multipurpose scheme also providing irrigation and drinking water. Khodri, Dhalipur, Dhakrani and Kulhal are further, smaller names worth recognising as part of the same Yamuna-Tons chain, though their individual capacities matter less than the river association itself.'),
      p('6. Agencies, benefits, and the environmental trade-off', 'must',
        'Three agencies dominate hydropower here, and matching project to agency is a common question type: UJVN Limited runs the state\'s own stations (Maneri Bhali, Chibro, Vyasi and others), THDC India Limited runs the Tehri complex plus Vishnugad Pipalkoti, and NHPC, the central public-sector company, runs Dhauliganga and Tanakpur. Beyond the electricity itself, well-run projects can offer irrigation, drinking water and flood moderation, plus the grid-balancing value pumped storage adds as renewable generation grows elsewhere in the system.',
        'But the same steep, geologically young terrain that makes hydropower so attractive also makes it risky: dam construction changes natural river flow and sediment movement, tunnelling and road-building disturb already-fragile slopes, reservoirs can mean submergence and displacement, several projects stacked on one river basin add up to a cumulative impact bigger than any single project\'s assessment usually captures, and the region\'s exposure to earthquakes, landslides and extreme rainfall makes dam safety an unusually serious concern here. The right way to approach hydropower questions is as a development-versus-environment trade-off, not a purely technical topic.'),
      s('7. Project → river → capacity → agency', 'must',
        'Tehri HPP → Bhagirathi → 1,000 MW (THDC); Tehri Complex total → 2,400 MW',
        'Koteshwar → Bhagirathi → 400 MW (THDC); Tehri PSP → 1,000 MW (THDC)',
        'Maneri Bhali I → Bhagirathi → 90 MW; Maneri Bhali II → Bhagirathi → 304 MW',
        'Vishnugad Pipalkoti → Alaknanda → 444 MW (THDC, under construction)',
        'Dhauliganga → Dhauliganga river → 280 MW (NHPC); Tanakpur → Sharda/Mahakali → 94.2 MW (NHPC)',
        'Vyasi → Yamuna → 120 MW (UJVN); Lakhwar → Yamuna → 300 MW (under development)',
        'Chibro → Tons → 240 MW (UJVN)'),
      s('8. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Tehri Dam is on the Bhagirathi, not the Alaknanda.',
        'Tehri HPP alone is 1,000 MW; the full Tehri Complex (with Koteshwar and the PSP) is 2,400 MW.',
        'Dhauliganga Power Station is on the Dhauliganga, not the Yamuna system.',
        'Chibro is on the Tons (a Yamuna tributary), not directly on the Yamuna.',
        'Run-of-river does not mean zero environmental impact.',
        'Under-construction status changes; attach a date to any such claim.'),
      s('9. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Tehri = Bhagirathi = 1,000 MW; Complex = 2,400 MW. Koteshwar = Bhagirathi = 400 MW. Tehri PSP = 1,000 MW. Maneri Bhali = Bhagirathi. Dhauliganga = Pithoragarh = 280 MW (NHPC). Tanakpur = Sharda (NHPC). Vyasi + Lakhwar = Yamuna (UJVN). Chibro = Tons. Vishnugad Pipalkoti = Alaknanda (THDC).')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused hydropower notes prepared from current UJVN Limited, THDC India Limited and NHPC project information. Project status and installed capacity should be rechecked near the exam because commissioning and construction status can change.'
  },

  'Industries & Transport': {
    priority: 'HIGH', title: 'Industries & Transport of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Industry and transport both follow the plains-versus-hills divide: large manufacturing and railways concentrate in Haridwar, Udham Singh Nagar and Dehradun, while the hill districts depend on roads. Focus on the named SIIDCUL industrial estates and their specialisations, and on transport gateways.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Why industry concentrates in the plains', 'must',
        'Industrial development in Uttarakhand follows the same plains-versus-hills divide that runs through the rest of its economic geography. Large manufacturing concentrates almost entirely in Haridwar, Udham Singh Nagar and Dehradun, for straightforward reasons: flat land is scarce everywhere else, road and rail connectivity is far better down here, these districts sit closer to Delhi-NCR and the wider north Indian market, and industrial infrastructure, labour, power and logistics are all easier to arrange. The mountain districts, by contrast, suit a different kind of activity altogether, horticulture processing, herbs, handicrafts, tourism and small-scale industry, rather than large manufacturing.'),
      p('2. SIIDCUL and its specialised estates', 'must',
        'SIIDCUL, the State Infrastructure and Industrial Development Corporation of Uttarakhand Limited, is the agency behind most of this plains industrial growth, having developed estates at Haridwar, Pantnagar, Sitarganj, Kashipur, Selaqui, Kotdwar and Dehradun. Each location has its own specialisation worth knowing individually: Haridwar\'s Integrated Industrial Estate hosts pharmaceuticals, consumer goods, electrical goods and general engineering; Pantnagar and Rudrapur, both in Udham Singh Nagar, form the state\'s strongest automobile and auto-component manufacturing belt alongside food processing and consumer industries; Sitarganj, in the same district, has developed around a Plastic Park; Kashipur combines general manufacturing with paper and agro-based industry and an Aroma Park for aromatic-plant processing; Selaqui, near Dehradun, is the state\'s clearest pharmaceutical cluster, sometimes called Pharma City, though Haridwar also has pharmaceutical units of its own; and Dehradun itself is the principal centre for IT and knowledge services, backed by the city\'s educational institutions and skilled workforce.'),
      p('3. Other industry types worth knowing', 'understand',
        'Beyond these named clusters, a few sector types are worth recognising on their own. Food processing draws directly on the state\'s cereals, fruit, vegetables, milk and horticultural output, with Udham Singh Nagar the strongest base and cold-chain infrastructure the key bottleneck for perishable produce. Forest and paper-based industry, historically strong around Kashipur, must now work within forest-conservation regulation rather than assuming raw timber access. And across the hill districts, cottage and traditional industries, woollen products, handloom, handicrafts, woodcraft, ringal (bamboo) products and local foods, provide the employment that large manufacturing cannot, using local skill and relatively little raw material; these link the state\'s economy directly to its culture and rural livelihoods, in a way the plains industrial belt does not.'),
      p('4. Roads: the backbone of hill connectivity', 'must',
        'Transport, like industry, is shaped directly by relief: roads dominate across the hill districts, railways stay concentrated in the plains and foothills, and air transport fills the gaps at important gateways and remote locations, with all three mattering for tourism, pilgrimage, defence and disaster response alike. Roads reach villages, pilgrimage centres and border areas that nothing else can, and mountain roads typically follow river valleys because valleys offer the easiest natural routes through otherwise rugged terrain, even though construction itself is difficult and constantly threatened by landslides, erosion and snowfall. National highways link the state to Delhi, Uttar Pradesh and Himachal Pradesh, with one major corridor running Dehradun-Haridwar-Rishikesh into the Garhwal Himalaya and another connecting the Kumaon plains to Nainital, Almora, Pithoragarh and the border areas; exact highway numbers change with renumbering and upgrades, so it is the routes themselves that are worth learning, not the numbers. Road access to the Char Dham, Badrinath, Kedarnath, Gangotri and Yamunotri, carries particular weight, supporting pilgrimage, tourism and defence logistics all at once, though widening these roads raises its own slope-stability and landslide concerns. The Uttarakhand Transport Corporation, the state\'s road-transport undertaking, has run both intrastate and interstate bus services since it began operating in 2003, after the reorganisation that followed statehood.'),
      p('5. Railways and their gateways', 'must',
        'Because high Himalayan terrain makes railway construction difficult, rail connectivity stays concentrated in the plains and foothills, with Dehradun, Haridwar, Rishikesh, Roorkee, Kotdwar, Ramnagar, Haldwani, Kathgodam and Lal Kuan as the important railheads, each acting as a gateway from which travellers continue by road into the mountains: Haridwar and Rishikesh for Garhwal and the Char Dham routes, Kotdwar toward Pauri Garhwal, Kathgodam and Haldwani as the main entry to Kumaon, Ramnagar for the Corbett region, and Lal Kuan as a key junction in the Kumaon plains. The one project extending this network deeper into the hills is the Rishikesh-Karnaprayag railway, which needs extensive tunnelling and bridging to push into the Garhwal Himalaya and matters for pilgrimage, tourism and strategic connectivity alike; its completion status is a current-affairs question worth checking close to the exam rather than treating as fixed.'),
      p('6. Air transport, and the disaster and strategic dimension', 'understand',
        'Air connectivity fills in where roads and railways can\'t reach quickly enough: Jolly Grant, near Dehradun, is the main commercial airport for the Garhwal region; Pantnagar Airport serves the Kumaon plains, in Udham Singh Nagar; and Naini Saini, in Pithoragarh, connects the eastern Himalayan region. Gauchar in Chamoli and Chinyalisaur in Uttarkashi function as smaller airstrips, and helicopter services carry particular weight for pilgrimage traffic and reaching remote mountain areas that fixed-wing flights cannot.',
        'Because so much of this network runs through unstable terrain, transport and disaster risk are closely linked here: monsoon landslides regularly cut mountain roads, flash floods damage bridges built along river valleys, and heavy snowfall can close high-altitude routes and passes altogether, even as the same roads remain essential for evacuation and relief once a disaster hits. The border districts, Uttarkashi, Chamoli, Pithoragarh and others facing China and Nepal, carry an added strategic weight, since road and air connectivity there serves defence needs as much as civilian ones.'),
      s('7. What to memorize', 'must',
        'SIIDCUL estates: Haridwar (general manufacturing), Pantnagar/Rudrapur (automobiles), Sitarganj (Plastic Park), Kashipur (Aroma Park + paper/agro), Selaqui (pharmaceuticals), Dehradun (IT/services)',
        'Roads dominate the hills; railways concentrate in the plains/foothills',
        'Railway gateways: Haridwar/Rishikesh → Garhwal; Kathgodam/Haldwani → Kumaon; Ramnagar → Corbett; Kotdwar → Pauri Garhwal',
        'Airports: Jolly Grant → Dehradun; Pantnagar → Udham Singh Nagar; Naini Saini → Pithoragarh'),
      s('8. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Pantnagar is in Udham Singh Nagar district, not Nainital.',
        'Selaqui\'s stronger association is pharmaceuticals, not automobiles (that is Pantnagar).',
        'Do not confuse Sitarganj\'s Plastic Park with Kashipur\'s Aroma Park.',
        'Do not confuse Jolly Grant (Dehradun) with Pantnagar Airport (Udham Singh Nagar).',
        'Heavy industry is not evenly spread across the state; it is concentrated in the plains and foothills.'),
      s('9. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Industry concentrates in plains + foothills. Haridwar = SIIDCUL manufacturing. Pantnagar/Rudrapur = automobiles. Sitarganj = Plastic Park. Kashipur = Aroma Park. Selaqui = pharma. Dehradun = IT/services. Hills depend on roads; railways stay in the plains/foothills. Jolly Grant = Dehradun; Pantnagar Airport = Udham Singh Nagar; Haridwar/Rishikesh and Kathgodam/Haldwani = the two main railway gateways.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused notes prepared from current Invest Uttarakhand/SIIDCUL and Uttarakhand government transport material. Industrial-park status, road statistics and major infrastructure-project completion status should be checked again near the examination because they can change.'
  },

  'National Parks & Wildlife': {
    priority: 'MUST KNOW', title: 'National Parks, Wildlife & Protected Areas of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Uttarakhand\'s biodiversity ranges from Terai-Shiwalik forest to alpine Himalaya. Focus on the six national parks (which district, which species), the tiger and elephant reserves, the key wildlife sanctuaries, and matching species to altitude.',
    sections: [
      p('1. Why the state is so biodiversity-rich', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand\'s huge altitude range, from tropical Terai and Bhabar right up to high alpine Himalaya, packs in an unusually wide set of ecosystems: tropical and subtropical forest, temperate forest, sub-alpine forest, alpine meadow, rivers and wetlands, which is why the state supports both lowland species like elephants and tigers and high-altitude species like the snow leopard and Himalayan musk deer within the same borders. Six national parks anchor this system and are worth learning as a fixed list before moving on to anything else: Jim Corbett, Rajaji, Nanda Devi, Valley of Flowers, Gangotri and Govind.'),
      p('2. Corbett and Rajaji: the lowland pair', 'must',
        'Corbett, established in 1936 as Hailey National Park, is recognised as the first national park of the Asian mainland, lies mainly across Nainital and Pauri Garhwal along the Ramganga river, and is known above all for its Bengal tigers, Asian elephants and rich birdlife. It also launched Project Tiger on 1 April 1973, making it central to India\'s tiger-conservation history, and today anchors the wider Corbett Tiger Reserve landscape, which includes the Sonanadi Wildlife Sanctuary. Rajaji National Park, spread across Dehradun, Haridwar and Pauri Garhwal in the Shiwalik foothills, is the state\'s other major lowland landscape, especially important for Asian elephants alongside tigers, and became Rajaji Tiger Reserve as India\'s 48th notified tiger reserve — making Corbett and Rajaji the two conservation anchors of Uttarakhand\'s lower Himalayan and Shiwalik belt.'),
      p('3. Nanda Devi and Valley of Flowers: the Chamoli/UNESCO pair', 'must',
        'Two more national parks sit together in Chamoli district and share a single UNESCO World Heritage listing. Nanda Devi National Park surrounds the Nanda Devi massif in genuinely rugged high-altitude terrain, home to snow leopard, Himalayan musk deer and bharal, and forms the core of the Nanda Devi Biosphere Reserve, established in 1988 as India\'s second Biosphere Reserve — a different designation from a national park, and worth keeping distinct. Valley of Flowers National Park, reached through the Govindghat-Ghangaria trekking route, is known for its high-altitude alpine meadows and exceptional flowering-plant diversity, and it is the pairing of these two parks, one raw wilderness, one alpine meadow, that makes up the UNESCO property.'),
      p('4. Gangotri and Govind: the Uttarkashi pair', 'must',
        'Uttarkashi district hosts the remaining two national parks. Gangotri National Park protects the high Himalayan terrain around the Gangotri Glacier and the upper Bhagirathi catchment, including Gaumukh itself, with wildlife such as snow leopard, bharal and other high-altitude Himalayan ungulates. Govind National Park, part of the wider Govind Pashu Vihar protected landscape in the same district, covers high Himalayan forest and alpine meadow around the Har-ki-Dun and upper Tons region. Between these four highland parks and the two lowland ones, Chamoli and Uttarkashi stand out as the state\'s key high-Himalayan protected-area districts.'),
      p('5. Wildlife sanctuaries', 'must',
        'A second tier of protected areas, wildlife sanctuaries, fills in the gaps between the national parks, and the names worth recognising are Kedarnath, Askot, Binsar, Govind Pashu Vihar, Sonanadi, Nandhaur and Benog. Kedarnath Wildlife Sanctuary, spanning Rudraprayag and Chamoli, and Askot Wildlife Sanctuary, in Pithoragarh near the international border, are both closely tied to Himalayan musk deer conservation across temperate, sub-alpine and alpine habitat. Binsar Wildlife Sanctuary, in Almora, protects temperate oak and rhododendron forest and is especially known for its bird diversity. Sonanadi, part of the wider Corbett Tiger Reserve landscape, and Nandhaur, in Kumaon, both sit in Terai-Shiwalik habitat and matter as much for the wildlife corridors they preserve, connecting separated forest blocks for elephants and tigers, as for the sanctuaries themselves.'),
      p('6. Conservation reserves and the elephant reserve', 'must',
        'A different, more recent category, the Conservation Reserve, protects government land valued for its landscape, corridor or habitat role rather than needing the stronger legal status of a park or sanctuary, and Uttarakhand has historical significance here: Asan Conservation Reserve (Dehradun, around the Asan river/barrage on the Yamuna system) and Jhilmil Jheel Conservation Reserve (Haridwar) were the country\'s first two conservation reserves. Asan protects wetland habitat important for migratory waterbirds and later gained Ramsar recognition as a wetland of international importance; Jhilmil Jheel protects wetland and grassland and is specifically tied to swamp deer (barasingha) conservation. Elephants get their own dedicated landscape too: the Shivalik Elephant Reserve, declared in 2002 and covering about 5,180 sq km, brings together Corbett, Rajaji, Sonanadi and the forest divisions around them specifically to protect elephant habitat and movement corridors.'),
      p('7. Matching species to altitude, and why corridors matter', 'must',
        'Matching species to altitude is one of the most reliable ways to answer questions here even without perfect recall of every park: tiger and Asian elephant belong to the Terai-Shiwalik forests at the bottom, swamp deer to wetlands and grasslands like Jhilmil, Himalayan musk deer to sub-alpine and high-Himalayan forest, Himalayan monal (the state\'s iconic pheasant) to temperate and sub-alpine zones, bharal to alpine terrain, and snow leopard, which preys on bharal, to the highest alpine and trans-Himalayan landscape of all. None of this protection works in isolation, though: wide-ranging animals like elephants and tigers regularly move outside park and sanctuary boundaries, so the corridors connecting separate forest blocks matter as much as the protected areas themselves, and roads, railways and settlements that cut across these corridors both fragment habitat and increase the human-wildlife conflict, crop damage from elephants, wild boar and monkeys, occasional livestock loss to large carnivores, that comes with people and wildlife living closer together as settlements expand.'),
      s('8. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Corbett: 1936, first national park, Ramganga, tiger; Project Tiger launched here, 1 April 1973',
        'Rajaji: Shiwalik, elephant + tiger, 48th tiger reserve',
        'Nanda Devi + Valley of Flowers: Chamoli, UNESCO World Heritage; Nanda Devi Biosphere Reserve, 1988, India\'s 2nd',
        'Gangotri NP: Uttarkashi, Gangotri Glacier/Bhagirathi; Govind NP: Uttarkashi, Har-ki-Dun/Tons',
        'Kedarnath + Askot WLS: musk deer; Binsar WLS: Almora, birds',
        'Asan CR: Dehradun, wetland, Ramsar; Jhilmil Jheel CR: Haridwar, swamp deer — India\'s first two conservation reserves',
        'Shivalik Elephant Reserve: 2002, Corbett + Rajaji + Sonanadi'),
      s('9. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Corbett National Park and Corbett Tiger Reserve are not the same thing; the reserve is the larger landscape.',
        'Nanda Devi and Valley of Flowers are in Chamoli, not Uttarkashi; Gangotri National Park is in Uttarkashi, not Chamoli.',
        'Askot Wildlife Sanctuary is in Pithoragarh, not Almora; Binsar Wildlife Sanctuary is in Almora, not Nainital.',
        'A Biosphere Reserve, a National Park and a UNESCO World Heritage Site are three different designations, not interchangeable labels.',
        'Snow leopard belongs to the high Himalaya, not the Terai; elephants belong to the lower forests, not the alpine zone.'),
      s('10. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        'Corbett = 1936 + tiger + Ramganga; Project Tiger 1973. Rajaji = Shiwalik + elephants. Nanda Devi + Valley of Flowers = Chamoli + UNESCO. Gangotri = Uttarkashi + glacier. Govind = Uttarkashi. Kedarnath + Askot = musk deer. Binsar = Almora + birds. Asan = Ramsar wetland. Jhilmil = swamp deer. Low altitude = tiger/elephant. High altitude = musk deer/bharal/snow leopard.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused protected-area and wildlife notes prepared using Uttarakhand Biodiversity Board and Uttarakhand Tourism/government material. Wildlife-population counts and rankings are intentionally excluded because they change with each census and should be revised through current affairs close to the examination.'
  },

  'Population & Settlements': {
    priority: 'HIGH', title: 'Population & Settlements of Uttarakhand',
    summary: 'Population is spread very unevenly because of relief, altitude and transport. Focus on the Census 2011 baseline figures, the hill-plains contrast, the major urban centres, and how settlement form itself changes between plains, valleys and hills.',
    sections: [
      p('1. The Census 2011 baseline', 'must',
        'The most recent full population count for Uttarakhand is Census 2011, and any figure below should carry that year attached to it, not be treated as current: total population 1,00,86,292 (51,37,773 male, 49,48,519 female), density 189 persons per sq km, sex ratio 963 females per 1,000 males, and literacy about 79.63% overall, split unevenly between male literacy (about 88.33%) and female literacy (about 70.70%).'),
      p('2. Why population is so unevenly spread', 'must',
        'Where people actually live follows the same relief pattern as everything else in this state: flat land, roads, jobs and industry pull population toward the plains, valleys and foothills, while steep slopes, harsh winters and limited farmland thin it out in the high mountains, and this shows clearly in the district figures — Haridwar had the largest district population in 2011, followed by Dehradun and then Udham Singh Nagar, while Rudraprayag had the smallest. Migration has sharpened this contrast further, pulling people from the interior hill districts toward these same plains and urban centres, so that population density effectively rises the closer you get to the plains and falls the further you go into the mountains.'),
      p('3. The major urban centres', 'must',
        'Dehradun, the state capital, sits in the Doon Valley between the Shiwalik and Lesser Himalayan ranges and has grown rapidly on the back of administration, education and services, making it the state\'s largest and most important urban centre. Haridwar, where the Ganga leaves the Himalayan foothills for the plains, combines religious, industrial and transport importance and belongs to the district with the highest overall population. In Kumaon, Haldwani, together with the adjoining railway town of Kathgodam, functions as the main gateway from the plains to Nainital and the wider Kumaon hills, while Rudrapur, in Udham Singh Nagar, has grown into a major industrial and urban centre on the back of the Pantnagar-Rudrapur manufacturing belt. Roorkee (education, industry, transport) and Kashipur (industry and commerce) are further important towns, and Rishikesh matters as a pilgrimage, yoga and tourism centre that also serves as the gateway into Garhwal.'),
      p('4. How settlement form itself changes with terrain', 'understand',
        'Settlement form changes as sharply with terrain as population density does. Plains settlements are large and compact, supported by level land and easy transport. Hill settlements, by contrast, tend to be scattered or strung out in a line, shaped by where the springs are, how stable the slope is, and how close a road runs, and are usually surrounded by the agricultural terraces that support them; very high elevations barely have permanent settlement at all, given the climate and terrain. River valleys sit in between: their relatively flatter land has long attracted towns, especially at valley junctions and river confluences, but that same valley-floor location also exposes settlements to floods and flash floods, which is why choosing where to build in the hills is really a trade-off between accessibility and hazard risk, not accessibility alone.'),
      p('5. Urbanisation, migration and seasonal pressure', 'understand',
        'Uttarakhand remained mostly rural in 2011, with an urbanisation rate of about 30.2% and an urban population of roughly 30.5 lakh, but urban growth has consistently outpaced rural growth since, concentrated in Dehradun, Haridwar and the industrially developed southern belt, with Rudrapur, Roorkee, Kashipur and Haldwani as the fastest-growing secondary centres. Migration is the biggest force reshaping this picture: people move from remote villages toward nearby towns, the plains, or out of the state entirely, chasing jobs, education and healthcare, which is why some interior hill villages have lost so much population they are popularly called "ghost villages," even though this pattern is far from universal and varies a great deal locally (the fuller picture is covered separately under Migration). Two further pressures are worth keeping distinct from permanent population: tourism and pilgrimage bring large seasonal surges to places like Haridwar, Rishikesh, the Char Dham towns, Nainital and Mussoorie, straining transport and infrastructure without actually adding residents, and settlements built on steep slopes or close to rivers, especially where construction has outpaced planning, face real landslide and flood risk that ties population geography directly back to disaster management.'),
      s('6. What to memorize', 'must',
        'Census 2011: population 1,00,86,292; density 189/sq km; sex ratio 963; literacy about 79.63%; urbanisation about 30.2%',
        'Highest population district: Haridwar; lowest: Rudraprayag',
        'Major urban centre: Dehradun (capital, Doon Valley); industrial urban belt: Haridwar + Udham Singh Nagar',
        'Kumaon gateway: Haldwani/Kathgodam'),
      s('7. Exam traps', 'trap',
        'Do not present Census 2011 figures as current population.',
        'Haridwar, not Dehradun, had the highest district population in 2011.',
        'Do not confuse seasonal tourist/pilgrim numbers with permanent resident population.',
        'Do not assume every hill village is losing population — the pattern varies locally.'),
      s('8. 30-second revision', 'revision',
        '2011 population = 1.0086 crore; density = 189; sex ratio = 963; literacy ≈ 79.63%; urbanisation ≈ 30.2%. Highest population district = Haridwar; lowest = Rudraprayag. Plains = dense + urban + industrial. Hills = sparse + scattered + migration-affected. Dehradun = dominant urban centre.')
    ],
    sourceNote: 'UKPSC-focused population and settlement notes based mainly on Census 2011 and Uttarakhand Government urban-development material. Census figures are explicitly treated as 2011 data; current population estimates should be handled separately under current affairs.'
  }
};
